---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const title = 'Data science vs software engineering career India: the honest 2026 comparison';
const description =
  'Data science vs software engineering career India compared on real pay, entry difficulty, degree needs, AI risk, and who actually wins each lane in 2026.';
const canonical =
  'https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/career-options/data-science-vs-software-engineering-career-india';
const publishedDateISO = '2026-07-05';
const publishedDateLabel = '5 July 2026';
const authorName = 'Shivanshi Sehgal';
const authorUrl = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/about/shivanshi-sehgal/';
const authorRole = 'Career researcher and writer';

const jumpLinks = [
  { id: 'short-answer', label: 'The short answer' },
  { id: 'what-the-work-is', label: 'What the daily work actually looks like' },
  { id: 'salary-reality', label: 'Salary reality: fresher to 5 years' },
  { id: 'demand-reality', label: 'Which one has more real demand right now' },
  { id: 'oversupply', label: 'The oversupply trap in data science' },
  { id: 'degree-path', label: 'Degree and entry path: what you actually need' },
  { id: 'ai-impact', label: 'How AI is changing both roles' },
  { id: 'checkpoint', label: 'The 4-Checkpoint Protocol for this decision' },
  { id: 'who-fits-swe', label: 'Who genuinely fits software engineering' },
  { id: 'who-fits-ds', label: 'Who genuinely fits data science' },
  { id: 'hybrid-paths', label: 'The hybrid paths nobody mentions' },
  { id: 'gates', label: 'The 3 Gates before you commit' },
  { id: 'switching-later', label: 'Can you switch between them later?' },
  { id: 'mistakes', label: 'Mistakes to avoid' },
  { id: 'next-step', label: 'What to do next' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQs' },
];

const workRows = [
  {
    aspect: 'Core daily task',
    swe: 'Design, build, test, and ship software systems: writing code, fixing bugs, reviewing pull requests, and maintaining production systems.',
    ds: 'Frame a business question, clean messy data, build and validate a model, and explain what it means to people who do not read code.',
  },
  {
    aspect: 'Where the day gets hard',
    swe: 'Debugging a failure that only shows up in production, or holding a large system\'s logic in your head across a long feature.',
    ds: 'Data that is missing, mislabeled, or contradictory, and a stakeholder who wants a confident answer from an uncertain model.',
  },
  {
    aspect: 'Main tools',
    swe: 'One deep language (Java, Python, JavaScript, Go), Git, cloud basics, system design, databases.',
    ds: 'Python or R, SQL, statistics, pandas/scikit-learn, visualization tools, and increasingly cloud ML platforms.',
  },
  {
    aspect: 'Who you talk to most',
    swe: 'Other engineers, product managers, and QA — mostly technical conversations about how something should work.',
    ds: 'Business teams, product owners, and leadership — mostly translating uncertain numbers into a decision someone can act on.',
  },
  {
    aspect: 'Where the ceiling comes from',
    swe: 'System design depth, cloud/DevOps skill, and ownership of complex, high-traffic systems.',
    ds: 'Model quality, business framing skill, and the ability to move an idea from a notebook into something that runs live.',
  },
];

const salaryRows = [
  {
    stage: 'Fresher, 0-1 years',
    swe: 'Rs 3.5-8 LPA at most services and mid-tier product firms; Rs 15-18 LPA+ only at a small slice of Tier-1 campuses.',
    ds: 'Rs 6-10 LPA on average; Rs 4-6 LPA for weak-portfolio bootcamp entrants, Rs 12-15 LPA+ for strong-portfolio candidates from top colleges.',
  },
  {
    stage: '2-4 years experience',
    swe: 'Rs 8-18 LPA depending on services vs product company, with a common 50-100% jump when switching from services to product.',
    ds: 'Rs 12-22 LPA, often ahead of an equivalent-experience software engineer, but with far fewer openings to compete for.',
  },
  {
    stage: '5+ years, senior',
    swe: 'Rs 18-30 LPA+, with staff/principal engineer tracks at product companies and GCCs going well beyond this.',
    ds: 'Rs 15-30 LPA+, with specialised GenAI/LLM data scientists earning 30-50% more than traditional data science peers at the same level.',
  },
  {
    stage: 'Applicant competition per opening (entry level, metro)',
    swe: 'High, but spread across a far larger absolute number of open roles nationwide.',
    ds: 'Roughly 280 applicants per entry-level opening in metro cities, up from about 90 in 2021, concentrated in a much smaller pool of roles.',
  },
];

const demandRows = [
  {
    signal: 'Total open roles on major job boards',
    reality:
      'Naukri and other large boards typically list tens of thousands of open software developer roles against a few hundred to low thousands of dedicated data scientist roles at any given time — software engineering has far more total openings.',
  },
  {
    signal: 'Growth rate of the smaller pool',
    reality:
      'AI and machine learning hiring rose roughly 25-45% year-on-year through parts of FY26 even as general IT hiring stayed flat or dipped slightly, so data science-adjacent demand is growing faster off a much smaller base.',
  },
  {
    signal: 'Where the shortage actually is',
    reality:
      'Industry estimates put India short by 230,000+ specialised data science and AI professionals even as roughly 600,000 data-science-oriented graduates enter the market each year against about 180,000 genuinely relevant entry openings — the shortage is in specialised, senior-capable talent, not entry-level headcount.',
  },
  {
    signal: 'GCC and product company hiring mix',
    reality:
      'Global Capability Centres in India, employing roughly 2.4 million people and still expanding, increasingly hire for AI, cloud, platform engineering, and applied data science together rather than treating them as separate tracks.',
  },
];

const checkpointItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Work style',
    body: 'Software engineering rewards people who can sit with a stubborn bug for hours and enjoy building something that runs. Data science rewards people who enjoy sitting with ambiguous, messy numbers and are comfortable saying "I am 70% confident" instead of a clean yes or no. Both need long, quiet focus — the difference is whether you get energy from building systems or from finding patterns and stories in data.',
    note: 'If you need a confident, binary answer at the end of every task, the probabilistic nature of data science will frustrate you daily.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Context',
    body: 'Can your family runway absorb a services-level fresher salary (Rs 3.5-8 LPA) for 2-3 years while you build proof in either lane? Data science freshers without a strong portfolio often start lower than software engineering freshers, not higher, despite the online hype about "data scientist" pay.',
    note: 'The headline salary comparisons you see online are usually the strong-outcome numbers, not the typical first-job number.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Market',
    body: 'Software engineering has a much larger absolute number of open roles; data science has a smaller, faster-growing pool with far more applicants per opening at entry level. More roles with more competition per role (software engineering) is a different risk profile from fewer roles with even more competition per role (data science).',
    note: 'Neither market is "easy." One is a wider funnel with more total winners; the other is a narrower funnel with a higher bar to clear.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Survival',
    body: 'AI is squeezing the same layer in both careers: routine, templated work. In software engineering, that means boilerplate coding. In data science, that means basic descriptive analysis and simple model-building that generative AI tools can now do in minutes. The safer position in both lanes is becoming the person who frames the problem, checks the AI\'s output, and owns the final call.',
    note: 'The question is not "will AI take this job." It is "which layer of this specific role still needs a human to own the decision."',
  },
];

const degreeRows = [
  {
    route: 'B.Tech/B.E. Computer Science or IT',
    ds: 'A strong, direct path into data science if you add statistics, machine learning coursework, and real projects on top of the core CS foundation.',
    swe: 'The most direct, industry-preferred route. Covers data structures, systems, networking, and software engineering fundamentals.',
  },
  {
    route: 'B.Tech/B.Sc. with a Data Science or AI specialisation',
    ds: 'Built specifically for this lane: statistics, machine learning, and big data tools alongside programming. Narrower job options than plain CS if you decide the lane is not for you.',
    swe: 'Less common as a direct pipeline into pure software roles, though the programming foundation still transfers.',
  },
  {
    route: 'Any other degree (maths, statistics, commerce, economics, even arts) plus self-study',
    ds: 'A genuinely open door. Employers in India increasingly weigh a portfolio of real projects, SQL and Python skill, and applied reasoning over the degree label. Many working data scientists in India did not start in computer science.',
    swe: 'Possible but harder without formal CS training, since software engineering interviews still test data structures and system design fairly rigorously regardless of your degree.',
  },
  {
    route: 'No degree, self-taught plus certifications',
    ds: 'Weak on its own. Bootcamp certificates without a strong project portfolio now compete against 280 applicants per entry role and often start at Rs 4-7 LPA, well below the confident number bootcamp marketing repeats.',
    swe: 'Also weak on its own for most companies, though a deployed public project history and open-source contributions can partly substitute for a degree at smaller firms and startups.',
  },
];

const swFitItems = [
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You like making something work end to end',
    body: 'You get satisfaction from shipping a feature, seeing it run, and fixing what breaks. Data science can feel unsatisfying if you want that concrete, "it works now" feeling more often than a model-accuracy report gives you.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You want more total job openings to aim at',
    body: 'Software developer roles vastly outnumber dedicated data scientist roles on every major Indian job board. If you want a wider, more forgiving funnel of opportunities across more company types and cities, this is the safer volume play.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You are comfortable with definite, testable outcomes',
    body: 'Code either passes the test suite or it does not. If you prefer clearer pass/fail feedback loops over the more interpretive, "does this model actually help the business" question in data science, this lane suits your thinking style better.',
  },
];

const dsFitItems = [
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You are pulled toward the "why," not just the "how"',
    body: 'You want to know why sales dropped, not just build the dashboard that shows it dropped. If a raw dataset makes you curious about the story behind it rather than just the display of it, that instinct is the core of the job.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You can hold real math and statistics without flinching',
    body: 'Linear algebra, probability, and statistical reasoning are not optional extras here the way they can be in most software engineering roles. If you actively enjoyed this in school rather than tolerated it, that is a real signal.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You can explain an uncertain answer to someone who wants a certain one',
    body: 'A model rarely gives a clean yes. Translating "the model is 78% confident" into a decision a non-technical manager can act on is a communication skill as much as a technical one, and it is the skill that actually separates well-paid data scientists from stuck ones.',
  },
];

const hybridItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Machine Learning Engineer',
    body: 'Sits between both worlds: takes a data scientist\'s model and turns it into a system that runs reliably in production, at scale, under real traffic. Needs solid software engineering discipline plus enough ML understanding to not break the model\'s logic while deploying it.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Analytics Engineer / Data Engineer',
    body: 'Builds and maintains the data pipelines that feed both software systems and data science models. Heavier on SQL, data warehousing, and engineering discipline than on statistics, and currently one of the fastest-expanding roles inside the broader data space in India.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'AI Product Engineer',
    body: 'A software engineer who specialises in integrating AI/LLM features into real applications — prompt design, retrieval systems, evaluation pipelines — without needing the deep statistical background of a classic data scientist.',
  },
];

const mistakeItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Choosing data science only because of the salary screenshots you saw online',
    body: 'The Rs 20+ LPA data science numbers circulating online are usually senior, product-company, or GenAI-specialist outcomes, not typical fresher pay. Most data science freshers without a strong portfolio start at Rs 4-7 LPA, sometimes lower than a services-company software engineering offer.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Assuming a data science bootcamp certificate alone will get you hired',
    body: 'Entry-level data analyst and data scientist roles in Indian metros now draw roughly 280 applicants per opening, up from about 90 in 2021. A certificate with no deployed project, no real dataset, and no explained business outcome gets filtered out fast in that pool.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Picking software engineering only because it "has more jobs" without checking your own fit',
    body: 'More total openings does not mean an easier path for you personally if the daily reality of debugging code for hours genuinely drains you. Volume of opportunity and personal fit are two separate questions; answer both.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Treating the two paths as permanently separate',
    body: 'A meaningful share of working data scientists in India started as software engineers or data analysts and moved sideways once they built the statistics and modelling layer on top of their existing coding skill. The paths cross more than career-guide diagrams suggest.',
  },
  {
    num: '05',
    title: 'Ignoring AI\'s effect on the easy entry tasks in both lanes',
    body: 'Generative AI tools now handle a large share of routine coding and basic descriptive analysis in both fields. Choosing either path and then coasting on entry-level tasks alone is a weaker bet than it was five years ago in both lanes, not just one.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Data science vs software engineering career India: which pays more?',
    a: 'At the fresher stage, it is closer than online salary charts suggest. Software engineering freshers at services firms typically earn Rs 3.5-8 LPA, while data science freshers average Rs 6-10 LPA, but a large share of data science freshers without a strong portfolio start at Rs 4-7 LPA. Past 2-4 years of experience, data scientists with real modelling skill and a track record often out-earn equivalent-experience software engineers by 10-20%, and specialised GenAI/LLM data scientists earn 30-50% more again. The gap favours data science mainly for people who build genuine depth, not for everyone who enters the field.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which has more job openings in India, data science or software engineering?',
    a: 'Software engineering, by a wide margin. Major Indian job boards typically list tens of thousands of open software developer roles at any time against a few hundred to low thousands of dedicated data scientist roles. Data science demand is growing faster percentage-wise, but off a much smaller base, and entry-level data science roles now draw roughly 280 applicants per opening in metro cities.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need a computer science degree for data science in India?',
    a: 'No, but it helps. Data science is one of the more degree-flexible tech careers — commerce, statistics, mathematics, economics, and even arts graduates work as data scientists in India if they build strong Python, SQL, and statistics skills plus a real project portfolio. Software engineering interviews, by contrast, still test data structures and system design fairly rigorously, which makes a formal CS background more valuable there.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is data science oversaturated in India right now?',
    a: 'At the entry level, yes, in a specific way. India produces roughly 600,000 data-science-oriented graduates a year against about 180,000 genuinely relevant entry-level openings, and metro entry-level roles draw around 280 applicants each. At the same time, India faces a shortage of 230,000+ specialised, senior-capable data science and AI professionals. The oversupply is in generic-credential entry candidates; the shortage is in people with real modelling depth and a portfolio that proves it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can a software engineer switch to data science later, or vice versa?',
    a: 'Yes, and it happens often in both directions. Software engineers usually need 6-12 months of focused statistics, Python-for-data, and machine learning study plus 3 or more real deployed projects to make a credible switch into a hybrid role like machine learning engineer or analytics engineer. Data scientists moving toward software-heavy roles usually need to build production-grade coding discipline, since data science work often stays inside notebooks rather than shipped systems.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is AI going to replace data scientists or software engineers first?',
    a: 'Neither is being replaced wholesale, but the easy entry-level layer is shrinking in both. In software engineering, AI now writes a large share of routine, boilerplate code, and entry-level postings globally are down roughly 28% from 2022 peaks. In data science, generative AI tools now handle basic descriptive analysis and simple model-building that used to be a junior\'s first tasks. In both fields, the safer position is becoming the person who frames the problem, directs the AI tool, and checks its output for correctness, not the person who only does the routine task the AI can now do faster.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which is a better career for someone who is good at maths but not that interested in coding all day?',
    a: 'Data science, generally, though it still requires real coding competence in Python and SQL. The daily rhythm leans more on statistics, hypothesis testing, and business framing than on pure software architecture. If coding all day genuinely drains you but working with numbers and patterns energises you, that is a meaningful signal worth testing with a small project before committing years to either path.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are paid bootcamps or online certifications worth it for data science or software engineering?',
    a: 'Only the specific course, not the platform name, decides this. A strong course includes graded projects on real or messy datasets, working code review or mentor feedback, and a portfolio piece you can show in an interview, not just a certificate PDF. Before paying, check the instructor\'s actual industry background, how recently the syllabus was updated, independent reviews beyond the platform\'s own testimonials, and whether past learners report real interviews or offers. If the same syllabus is freely available through official documentation, YouTube channels from working practitioners, and open datasets, the paid version only earns its price if it adds structure, feedback, or accountability you would not otherwise get.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is an MS or M.Tech in data science worth doing, in India or abroad?',
    a: 'Test it against the same discipline you would apply to any large education spend: does the specific program\'s placement record, faculty research relevance, and cost justify itself, or is a portfolio of real projects plus targeted certifications a cheaper, faster route to the same outcome? A postgraduate degree makes clearer sense when a specific target role or research path genuinely requires it, for example a research-heavy applied-AI role, and less sense as a default response to feeling behind in the job market. If the total cost would consume a large share of your family\'s realistic education budget, build one strong project first and test the market with it before committing to the degree.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Data science vs software engineering career India comes down to one honest trade: software
    engineering gives you a much bigger, more forgiving job market; data science gives you a smaller,
    faster-growing market with a higher entry bar and often stronger long-term pay if you build real depth.</strong>
    Neither one is the "safe" choice and neither is the "hype" choice — both are being reshaped by AI at
    the same easy-entry layer, and in both, the people who actually win are the ones who build a genuine,
    provable <strong>skill portfolio</strong> instead of chasing the job title with the better-sounding
    salary screenshot. That decision, made correctly early, is what actually moves you toward stronger
    income opportunities and earlier financial freedom, not the label on your degree.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Software engineering has far more total open roles in India; data science has a smaller pool growing faster off a lower base.</li>
      <li>Fresher pay is closer than the internet makes it look: Rs 3.5-8 LPA for software engineering vs Rs 4-10 LPA for data science, with weak-portfolio data science freshers often starting lower, not higher.</li>
      <li>Entry-level data science and analyst roles now draw roughly 280 applicants per opening in Indian metros, up from about 90 in 2021 — the field looks glamorous but the entry filter is brutal.</li>
      <li>Data science is more degree-flexible; software engineering interviews still lean hard on formal data structures and system design knowledge.</li>
      <li><strong>Past 2-4 years, data scientists with real modelling depth often out-earn equivalent-experience software engineers by 10-20%, but that gap only shows up for people who build a genuine skill portfolio, not for everyone who enters the field.</strong></li>
      <li>Test your actual fit with one small project in each lane before locking in four years of college or a career switch.</li>
      <li>In either lane, domain knowledge is the multiplier. Add AI direction, communication, product or business judgment, and deployed proof; that stack raises the ceiling and can later support specialist consulting, freelancing, or a product.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you are still weighing this against other 12th-science or B.Tech options more broadly, read
    <a href="/blog/career-options/pcm-career-options/">PCM career options</a> for the wider decision first.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you want a clearer, faster read on which of these actually fits how you think and work, use the
    <a href="/career-skills-compass/">Career & Skills Compass</a> before spending years committing to either one.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to data science vs software engineering career India</h2>

  <p>
    There is no universal winner here, and anyone who tells you there is has not looked at both the salary
    data and the job-posting data at the same time.
  </p>

  <p>
    Software engineering wins on volume: more total roles, more companies hiring, more cities with real
    opportunities, and a clearer, more standardised interview process built around data structures and
    system design.
  </p>

  <p>
    Data science wins on ceiling, for the specific people who build real depth: stronger long-term pay for
    strong performers, faster-growing demand percentage-wise, and more direct exposure to business
    decision-making rather than pure technical execution.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Both fields are currently oversold by two different kinds of hype. Software engineering was oversold
      as "learn to code, guaranteed job" a decade ago. Data science is being oversold the same way right
      now, with six-figure salary screenshots and three-month bootcamp promises. The real answer sits in
      the boring middle for both: strong demand exists in each, a real entry filter exists in each, and
      your actual fit and proof of work decide who wins, not the field you pick on paper.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="what-the-work-is">What the daily work actually looks like in each career</h2>

  <p>
    Most people compare these two careers using job titles and salary numbers before they have ever looked
    at what the actual Tuesday afternoon looks like in each role. That is backwards.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Aspect of the work</th>
          <th>Software engineering</th>
          <th>Data science</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {workRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Aspect of the work"><strong>{item.aspect}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Software engineering">{item.swe}</td>
            <td data-label="Data science">{item.ds}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Notice the difference is not really "coding vs no coding." Data scientists code every day, usually in
    Python and SQL. The real difference is what the code is for: software engineering code builds and runs
    systems, data science code extracts and tests a pattern, then hands the finding to someone else to act
    on.
  </p>

  <h2 id="salary-reality">Salary reality: fresher to 5 years, without the marketing numbers</h2>

  <p>
    Salary comparison articles for this exact keyword tend to quote the best-case numbers for whichever
    field they are promoting. Here is the range you can actually expect at each stage, based on current
    salary-tracking sources and hiring reports.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Career stage</th>
          <th>Software engineering</th>
          <th>Data science</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {salaryRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Career stage"><strong>{item.stage}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Software engineering">{item.swe}</td>
            <td data-label="Data science">{item.ds}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Ranges are directional, based on current salary-tracking sources, hiring reports, and job-board data at
    the time of writing. Verify current figures against live postings before making a financial decision.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The "data science pays more" claim is true on average for people who reach 3-5 years with real
      modelling skill. It is not reliably true for freshers. A software engineering offer from a services
      company is a more predictable floor than a random data science bootcamp certificate, even though the
      data science ceiling can go higher for the people who actually build depth.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="demand-reality">Which one has more real demand right now</h2>

  <p>
    This is the part most comparison articles get lazy on. "Both are growing fields" is true and also
    useless, because the two markets are shaped completely differently.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Demand signal</th>
          <th>What it actually shows</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {demandRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Demand signal"><strong>{item.signal}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What it actually shows">{item.reality}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Software engineering market shape</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Wide funnel: tens of thousands of open roles across services, product, and startups.</li>
        <li>More predictable hiring process built around DSA and system design.</li>
        <li>More total winners, spread across a wider range of company types and pay bands.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Data science market shape</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Narrow funnel: a smaller number of dedicated roles, growing fast off a small base.</li>
        <li>Heavier weight on a strong project portfolio over a generic certificate.</li>
        <li>Fewer total roles, but a real shortage of people who can do the senior-level work well.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="oversupply">The oversupply trap hiding inside the data science hype</h2>

  <p>
    Here is the specific number that most "should I do data science" articles skip: India produces roughly
    600,000 data-science-oriented graduates every year against only about 180,000 genuinely relevant
    entry-level openings in the same period.
  </p>

  <p>
    At the same time, entry-level data analyst and junior data scientist roles in metro cities now attract
    an average of around 280 applicants per opening, up from about 90 applicants per opening in 2021.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      This is not a reason to avoid data science. It is a reason to stop treating a three-month bootcamp
      certificate as a job guarantee. The gap between "did a data science course" and "got a data science
      job" is now wider than the gap between "did a CS degree" and "got a software job," specifically
      because the entry credential got diluted faster than the entry filter got easier.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Meanwhile, India is separately projected to face a shortage of over 230,000 specialised data science
    and AI professionals. Both facts are true at once: too many generic entrants competing for junior
    roles, and too few people with real, senior-capable modelling depth. The oversupply is at the bottom of
    the funnel; the shortage is at the top.
  </p>

  <h2 id="degree-path">Degree and entry path: what you actually need for each</h2>

  <p>
    One of the biggest genuine differences between these two careers is how flexible the entry path is.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Entry route</th>
          <th>Data science</th>
          <th>Software engineering</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {degreeRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Entry route"><strong>{item.route}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Data science">{item.ds}</td>
            <td data-label="Software engineering">{item.swe}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Degrees have a place here — a B.Tech CS degree still opens the widest, most standard door into software
    engineering, and a strong quantitative degree genuinely helps in data science. But degree-only thinking,
    where someone collects the credential and stops there, fails harder in data science than it used to,
    because the market has enough credentialed candidates now that it filters on portfolio and applied
    reasoning instead.
  </p>

  <p>
    In both lanes, the real lever is the same one: a <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> that
    combines the right technical skill mix, visible proof of work, and the ability to communicate it,
    stacked on top of whichever degree you already have or are finishing. That portfolio, not the degree
    label, is what actually unlocks stronger income opportunities and moves you toward earlier financial
    freedom in either field.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-impact">How AI is changing both roles, not just one</h2>

  <p>
    A common mistake in this comparison is assuming AI threatens only software engineering ("AI writes
    code now") while data science stays safe. That is incomplete.
  </p>

  <p>
    Generative AI tools already write a large share of routine, boilerplate code, and global entry-level
    software postings are down roughly 28% from their 2022 peak. But the same tools also now handle basic
    descriptive analysis, simple visualisations, and first-pass model building — tasks that used to be a
    junior data analyst's entire first year of learning.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      In both fields, AI is shrinking the "easy entry task" layer and growing demand for the person who can
      frame the problem correctly, direct the AI tool, and check its output for correctness before anyone
      relies on it. That layer is not disappearing in either career. It is becoming the entire point of
      both careers.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Rather than asking "which career is safer from AI," a sharper question is: in whichever lane you pick,
    are you becoming the person who owns the judgment call, or the person doing the task AI is about to do
    faster than you?
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">Use The 4-Checkpoint Protocol before you commit to either path</h2>

  <p>
    A salary chart cannot tell you which one fits your actual life and thinking style. The 4-Checkpoint
    Protocol narrows this decision to what genuinely matters for you.
  </p>

